  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nThe dominant mid/upper-level high pressure continues to bring 100 \ndegree high temperatures to much of South Texas (with 90s along the \ncoastal zones). Coupled with dewpoint temperatures still remaining \nin the 70s in the afternoons east of a line from Cross to Benavides, \nthe HeatRisk is forecast to climb back to mostly Major (level 3 of \n4) with heat indices climbing to as high as 114F. For this reason, a \nHeat Advisory has been issued these zones from noon to 7 PM today.\n \nThis heat dome slides ever so slightly westward Thursday and Friday, \nresulting in upper-level winds to come out of the northwest/north \nallowing some disturbances to push into south-central Texas. While \nthe majority of CAMs have some form of composite reflectivity moving \nthrough the northern Brush Country Thursday evening, any shower that \nmoves in from the I-10 corridor will be elevated in nature leading \nto virga as any storms moves toward the Hwy 59 corridor. That said, \nstorms forming from the collision of the sea-breeze may be more \nefficient at dropping a few more raindrops (more so for the Victoria \nCrossroads as their PWATs are forecast to be between 2.0-2.2\"). \n\nFriday's rain chances (20-30% in the Victoria Crossroads and less so \nfurther south along the Coastal Bend) are a bit lower in confidence \namong the global models. In the absence any rain on Thursday and \nFriday, higher dewpoints would increase the HeatRisk and would \nwarrant further consideration of any heat-related headlines. Moving \nfurther into the forecast period, low end rain chances (<20%) \nassociated with the seabreeze return as the heat dome slides east \nand weakens as it does so. Summer in Texas is still in full force.",
